  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 216 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nBy Monday, the mid-level ridge that supported a multi-day period \nof potent northwesterly flow retrogrades westward, with shortwave \ntroughing beginning to sweep across the Southeast. Despite a turn \ntoward the slightly-less-benign aloft, high pressure remains in \nplace at the surface -- the resultant subsidence sufficient to \nsupport continued near-negligible rain chances through midweek. \nWith a supremely dry airmass in place, portions of central Georgia\nmay approach critical relative humidity thresholds on Monday and \nTuesday. \n\nMoisture will be on the upswing by Thursday, flow turning \nsoutheasterly at the surface as a subtropical high nudges onshore.\nAfter multiple days with PWATs lingering near climatological \nminimums (between 0.4-0.7\" -- at times well below the 10th \npercentile), expect them to be dragged back above an inch. Chances\nfor isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will return \nas the airmass moderates back toward summerlike, bringing an end \nto our brief dry period for some. \n\nWhile comparatively much drier air will linger (dewpoints in the \n40s to 50s),temperatures will warm back into the upper 80s to \nlower 90s next week -- some 5 to 12 degrees above average. Morning\nlows will remain crisp through Wednesday, in the 40s for portions\nof northeast Georgia at elevation, and in the 50s elsewhere. As \nmoisture rebounds midweek, lows will return to the 60s to near 70,\na whopping 10 to 20 degrees above average for early October (!).",
